Welcome. As your engineering consultant, I have reviewed the 16 attached resources (covering software engineering, patterns, security, and analysis) and understood the nature of your environment (ABP Framework with ASP.NET Core and Angular).
I will now formulate the index of ideas and concepts for this deep educational curriculum, which aims to build a solid engineering mindset before writing any line of code, with strict adherence to all the rules you set.
Here is the proposed lesson plan:
Curriculum Index: The Journey from Philosophy to Application in ABP & Angular Environment
Lesson 1: Core Clean Architecture & Separation of Concerns
General Concept: Synthesizing the philosophy of business rule Centrality from the Clean Architecture book
with the Layered Architecture concept from the Domain-Driven Design book
. We will discuss how Business Rules protect against infrastructure details.
Projection onto your environment: How ABP strictly applies the Dependency Rule through Domain, Application, and EntityFrameworkCore layers, and how the Angular application should treat APIs as abstract interfaces rather than mere data transporters.
Lesson 2: Strategic Design & Domain Modeling
General Concept: Exploring the concept of Bounded Contexts and Ubiquitous Language from Domain-Driven Design
, and the importance of understanding the problem before the solution from Systems Analysis and Design
.
Projection onto your environment: How to divide Modules in ABP based on Contexts rather than technical functions, and how to reflect business language inside TypeScript objects in Angular to ensure mental alignment between both teams.
Lesson 3: Tactical Patterns & Object Lifecycle
General Concept: Synthesizing the Repository, Factory, and Aggregates patterns from DDD
with Creational Patterns from Design Patterns
.
Projection onto your environment: When to use Managers and AppServices in ABP? And why do we use IRepository instead of direct database access? And how do we protect the State of Aggregates from arbitrary mutation via DTOs coming from Angular.
Lesson 4: Security by Design & Defense in Depth
General Concept: Synthesizing Input Validation and Session Management concepts from Web Application Hacker's Handbook
and OWASP Testing Guide
. Focusing on security as an architectural responsibility, not an add-on feature.
Projection onto your environment: ABP strategies in Authorization and Permissions, how to handle JWT in Angular, and protecting the API from Injection attacks via DTOs and automatic Validations.
Lesson 5: Integration & Decoupling
General Concept: The Depend on Abstractions principle from Clean Architecture
and the Favor Composition over Inheritance principle from Design Patterns
.
Projection onto your environment: Effectively using Dependency Injection in ASP.NET Core, and how to design Angular Services to be replaceable and testable without heavy reliance on Backend details.

