Lesson 1: Core Clean Architecture & Separation of Concerns
1. Abstract Engineering Concept (Synthesis)
The core idea agreed upon by major engineering references (Martin, Evans, Sommerville) is that software is not just working code, but a structure that must resist time.
The Dependency Rule: "Robert Martin" clarifies that the most important rule in Clean Architecture is that Dependencies must always point inwards, towards Business Rules
. This means the Domain code must not know anything about the database or web interface.
Isolating the Core from Details: "Eric Evans" in DDD and "Martin" distinguish between the Core Domain and Infrastructure details. The UI (like Angular) and databases (like SQL Server) are considered "details" that should be mere Plugins that can be replaced without breaking core business logic
.
Modularity & Coupling: Systems engineering books (Sommerville & Tilley) emphasize the importance of Low Coupling. You must design the system so that if you change something in the user interface, you don't have to change database code or business logic
.
2. Strategic Projection onto your environment (ABP, .NET, Angular)
How do you apply these deep theories within the ABP Framework with Angular?
Layers in ABP are not just for organization, but for protection:
In ABP, the Domain layer is the "heart". It must only contain Entities and business rules. It is strictly forbidden to use any technical libraries (like HttpContext or EntityFramework) inside this layer. This is a direct application of the Dependency Rule
.
The API as an Adapter:
The API interface (which you build with .NET) and the Angular application are mere Delivery Mechanisms
. Do not put complex business logic inside the Controller or inside Angular components. Angular's job is presentation only, and the Controller's job is to receive the request and pass it to the Application Layer.
The Danger of Leaked DTOs:
One of the biggest strategic mistakes is returning database Entities directly to Angular. This breaks isolation. You must always use Data Transfer Objects (DTOs). If you change a table in the database, the Angular application should not stop working; the DTO acts as a buffer protecting the Frontend from Backend changes
.
3. Performance Insight
Based on the attached SQL Server books, separation between layers does not mean neglecting performance. When you design the Domain Model, remember that the way data is called will affect Execution Plans
. Using an ORM (like EF Core in ABP) poorly can generate disastrous SQL queries (like the N+1 problem). Clean Architecture does not exempt you from understanding how your requests translate into SQL
.

